> Even if convenient for beginners, this looks very arbitrary.

Although my change slight help for early game character, I don't think
it will make the early game significantly easier. And as I said, I
don't think it's that arbitrary either. In case you're interested,
here are the relevant formulas. First, the original code (with
comments)

  int chance = rn2(20);
  int spell_bonus = type ? spell_hit_bonus(type) : 0;

  /* small chance for naked target to avoid being hit */
  if (!chance) return rnd(10) < ac+spell_bonus;

  /* very high armor protection does not achieve invulnerability */
  ac = AC_VALUE(ac);

  return (3 - chance) < ac+spell_bonus;

Now, my new code, with my comments. (I did some calculations and wrote
some comments while I was making the changes)

  int spell_bonus = type ? spell_hit_bonus(type) : 0;

  return rn2(25+spell_bonus) > rn2(dex);
  // With spell_bonus == 0
  // 74% @ dex=14, (early game dex)
  // 66% @ dex=18, (max natural dex)
  // 56% @ dex=23, (w/ +5 gauntlets of dex)
  // 43% @ dex=30, (level of arch-lich)

  // I think this is fair, compared to the previous system which was
basically just
  // 100% in the early game, 25% in the late game.
  // dex 18 in this system is roughly the same as ac -4 in the old,
which is not a really low ac.

By the way, this formula is used to determine whether or not monsters
get hit by beams as well. Originally it used the monster's AC level
(which was typically much worse that the player's AC level), now it
uses the monster's difficulty level in place of the player's dexterity
level.

